      ro: "",
      en: "Le Morne Brabant is a monumental basaltic mountain at the southwestern tip of Mauritius, rising 556 meters above the sea. The mountain is not only a geological landmark but also holds deep historical significance: in the 18th and 19th centuries, it was a refuge for runaway slaves (maroons) who found safety in the inaccessible caves and at the summit. For this reason, the mountain became a universal symbol of resistance against slavery and a monument to freedom, leading to its inclusion on the UNESCO World Heritage list in 2008. The lagoon at the base of the mountain is world-famous for kitesurfing, while the hike to the summit is one of the island's most thrilling experiences, though the final section is rocky and exposed. History K8 — Slavery and Resistance in Mauritius."
